Z||000000||phillip||04-12-2000||01:34 AM||||<BR>just got a new jc whitney.they are offering drilled and slotted brake rotors for 92-00 grand ams, sunbird, and sunfire. also 82-96 s-10s. would these work as part of a brake conversion?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Standard||04-12-2000||01:38 AM||||The grand am's won't.  I've heard somewhere about using rotors from the 4x4 s-10, but i think that requires fairly extensive modding.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ironhorse||04-12-2000||01:07 PM||||Phillip... Here is the information you're looking for.. this will tell you what you need, part you need, and how to make it works.  I'm thinking about doing this on my Fiero..  <A HREF="http://FieroZone.tripod.com/realbrakes/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://FieroZone.tripod.com/realbrakes/index.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Capt Fiero||04-12-2000||01:26 PM||||I can without doubt say that this is a good swap.  My work with grand am is a bit diffrent though.  I did the rear as it is a bolt on swap no mods at all, just un bolt fiero bolt on grand am rotors and calipers.  Now some of you are going to say why the rear and not the front.  Look at it this way.  GM has the proportioning valve set at 80/20 the fronts are working way to hard, we all have front wheel lock up problems, that is good and bad in a way.  Yes the front should lock up first. But not near as easy as they do in our cars.  I installed the grand am rear becuase it increases the rear stopping power by use of a larger piston in the caliper.  The largers rotors won't fade either. We have a ton more weight in the rear than the reg front drive buggers.  I drive my car hard, at the rears really pull the car to a stop better now. I can lock up the fronts still in a real panic on the wet or ice, but that is real hard to do, consedering I can now pull 1.2g's of braking force out of my car. At that rate of stopping I almost never have a problem and if I do abuse the brakes real bad and somehow manage to heat the fronts the rears are not heated as bad and I still have brakes.  THE BIG DOWN SIDE.  I have no rear parking brake now. My car is an auto so it is not a big thing. My parking brake never worked anyways.   In short the rears take away work from the front so the fronts do not heat up so bad and the rears help the car stop faster.  Sorry for the long winded post, but with my new 3.4 on the engine stand getting set up for the car I am going to have some real fun soon. ten thou over bore, and maybe a nice cam to help it breath better.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||04-12-2000||02:52 PM||||You can only use the 91 and earlier grand am/beratta rotors though, so those JC Whitney one's won't work..||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||04-12-2000||05:31 PM||||The Fiero Store offers a complete vented brake upgrade kit now for the fiero front brakes. I think they are the Beretta brakes. I just bought them and if you want to know how they work e-mail me. Check it out <A HREF="http://www.secureleadercom.com/fierostore/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.secureleadercom.com/fierostore/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||04-12-2000||05:32 PM||||There's something goofy about the 91 Grand Am brakes that don't work, but the 91 Beretta will. Basically just ask for a late 80's Grand Am or Beretta brakes. I bought all 88 Grand Am parts to make it simple and that worked ok for me.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||04-12-2000||07:27 PM||||I have to do some brake work soon. I'm likely going to do the GA front set.<P>On the rear I'm going to try something diferant. I'm going to leave the rear stock and eliminate the propotioning valve.<P>The whole point of the valve is to reduce rear brake power. Since the factory brakes are already weaker than the GA brakes, I think eliminating the prop valve will maintain the ballance while boosting rear brake power.<P>I'll let you all know what it works like. I don't have a fade problem since I don't race but the part cost is an issue. I'm also going to try Raybestos QS ceramic compound brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000008||jhar1987||04-12-2000||07:47 PM||||I did the berreta brake upgrade and it is the way to go. I can't believe the bite the car has on the road as compared to stock. It is well worth the effort.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||04-12-2000||10:17 PM||||Ogre, it's not often that I can caution you or give you advice, but I'd research that proportioning valve thing a little more. I don't specifically know about the Fiero, but my streetrodding experience has taught me that most factory brake setups use what is called a combination valve which is 2 or 3 different valves built into one unit. These valves are hold-off/metering valve, proportioning valve, and residual pressure valve. The hold-off/metering valve is only used in disc/drum brake systems (I think). But disc brakes need a 2 psi residual pressure valve (drum brakes use a 10 psi valve). Since the master cylinder is well above the calipers, you might not need the residual pressure valve, but on old trucks where the M/C is mounted down under the cab it's critical. I don't believe the proportioning valve is as important on a 4 wheel disc brake system as it is on a disc/drum setup, but you might still put an adjustable one in if your experiment doesn't work out correctly.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Capt Fiero||04-12-2000||10:26 PM||||Please for your own saftey do not kill the proportiaing valve.  YOU MUST USE IT. You can modify it, but do not remove it.  Simpluy unscrew the valve and install a lighter spring, if you remove it, you are going to hit the brakes on a corner and could end up head on with some long haul tractor.  THis is not a good thing.  I have spoke to several people about changing it.  One guy had swapped it around a bit and then while testing it, ended up facing the opisite direction quicker than he could blink, when the rear end of a Fiero decides to let go it does just that it lets go fast!  It is not the same as power sliding the car, that you can hit the gas back of the gas and keep it under semi conrol, when you have a brake enduced slide it can get very scary, if you panic you will just hit the brakes harder, in a panic situtation most do just that slam the brakes.  I am the king of saying the pro valve is wrong, but you must use it. 80/20 is bad we need 60/40. BUY A ADJUSTABLE VALVE for 60$.  I do not want to see a fellow fiero owner and his car in the wrecker. That is not a good thing.  <BR>David||||reg||1
Z||000011||lowCG||04-13-2000||01:06 PM||||CaptnFiero,<BR>I'll be using larger,four piston calipers,same size on all four wheels,so should the stock proportioning valve be pretty close?<BR>Or which springs did you find to work best in your system with your Grand Am brakes?<BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||04-13-2000||02:13 PM||||The fiero valve, like most cars is just a prop valve stuck in one side of the ballance switch block.<P>I'll see how it works out. The Fiero stock rear is significantly weaker to start with than the GA's are, even at full line pressure. I know if I use any valve at all I'll need an adjustable one. I'm not even going to waste the time on farting with springs. <P>As I've often said, I really don't like the elimination of the E-brake. I've had to use them on a couple occasions in other cars when master cylinders failed. It may take a year but that hand brake can and will stop the car. It is not just a "parking" brake like most people think. If you ever have a hydraulic problem that involves a wreck you'll find yourself on the very ugly end of a stick if an investigator finds the E-brake removed intentionally.||||reg||1
